  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three device architectures for transparent electrochromic systems. In all cases, the drawings are exploded views that show how the active layers are stacked between transparent conductors.\n\nThe left design is a conventional multilayer electrochromic device: two transparent conductive electrodes sandwich an electrochromic layer, an electrolyte, and a separate ion-storage layer. This layout emphasizes the need for both optical modulation and ionic charge balance across several thin films.\n\nThe middle design shows a related stack in which the lower functional layer is replaced by a metal anode. Here, the electrochromic layer and electrolyte remain, but the metal component appears to act as the counter electrode or ion source, simplifying the structure compared with the full five-layer stack.\n\nThe right design presents an even simpler concept. A Pt-modified transparent conductive electrode sits above an electrolyte, while a metal grid on a glass or PET substrate serves as the lower current-collecting structure. This suggests a move from fully continuous layered films toward a more open metal-grid support, likely intended to reduce complexity while maintaining transparency and conductivity. Overall, the figure highlights structural simplification from a conventional electrochromic sandwich to metal-assisted transparent devices.",
